drm/apple: stop a silent external DCP from pinning a Thunderbolt DP route

Local patch (not upstream), written 2026-10-22 against the #158 source plus
0001. Build-only; never booted. Evidence and reasoning:
mseries/aurora/hardware-tests/2026-10-22-j314s-wip3-dcpext1-dptx-wedge.md

On the J314S, the first dock plug of a boot can be routed to dcpext1
(28cc00000). Its AppleDCPDPTXRemotePort service then never answers: the link
validation times out, and so does every later call, including the release.
dcp_tb_release_locked() treats a refused release as "keep the route owned",
which is right for a refusal but wrong for a timeout: the port stays pinned to
the dead pipeline, and each new request on it first tries to release it again
and fails with -110 until reboot. Other ports are unaffected.

 - A DPTX call that times out marks that DCP's pipeline unresponsive for 300 s
   (cleared when a connect gets an answer).
 - An unresponsive pipeline is not offered to new tunnels or direct routes and
   is not planned onto, unless a tunnel already holds it. With no other
   pipeline free the request returns -EBUSY, so the Thunderbolt side waits and
   asks again (apple_dpin capacity_retry) and lands on dcpext0 meanwhile.
 - A release that times out is completed without the firmware: our view of the
   display is dropped and the crossbar route freed, instead of kept owned.

Not covered: moving a tunnel that already attached to the silent pipeline,
within the same plug, to another one. That needs the Thunderbolt side to
re-request the tunnel; see the evidence note.

